15.3  sudo and groups

TL;DR
  - sudo runs one command as root, if /etc/sudoers (or a file in
    /etc/sudoers.d/) allows it.
  - Usually one group gets that right: sudo on Debian and Ubuntu, wheel on
    Fedora. On Arch, you enable the wheel line yourself.
  - Group changes apply at your next login, not in shells already open.

      id                              # your user and groups
      sudo -l                         # what sudo lets you run
      sudo usermod -aG docker alice   # add alice to the docker group

On your machine
  User      alice (uid 1000, gid 1000)
  Groups    alice, wheel, docker, 4242
  Admin     member of wheel: the group sudo usually trusts
  Umask     0022: new files 644 (rw-r--r--), new folders 755

Pitfalls
  - usermod -G without -a replaces all your extra groups. Always -aG.
  - Edit sudoers with visudo: it checks the syntax, and a broken sudoers can
    lock everyone out of sudo.
  - Being in the docker group is as good as being root: whoever can talk to the
    Docker daemon can mount the whole disk into a container.
  - sudo echo x > /etc/file fails: the redirection runs in your shell, not as
    root. Use echo x | sudo tee /etc/file.
  - To use a new group without logging out, run newgrp docker in that shell.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 15.3 --why

Related
  Commands  sudo, id, usermod, visudo
  Paths     /etc/sudoers, /etc/sudoers.d/, /etc/group
  See also  idk 15.1 rwx, octal and umask
